# Loid Compiler/Transpiler Architecture

This document describes the high-level architecture of the Loid transpiler (and soon the compiler) and its development infrastructure.
This should serve as a guide for contributors and as a roadmap for future enhancements.

## Table of Contents

- [Overview](#overview)

## Overview

Loid is a statically-typed, multi-paradigm programming language.
At this moment, Loid compiles to JavaScript.

For the sake of organization it is important to highlight our architectural decisions:
- **Modularity**: each part of the language can be developed and tested as independently as possible.
- **Correctness**: the Rust code must be written in a clean manner, following the `rustfmt.toml` file with `rustfmt`, and organized and documented clearly.
- **Extensibility**: new compilation targets must be capable of being added without being forced to re-write too much the core logic.

## Compilation Pipeline

The compiler processes source files through a very linear pipeline.

```bash
Source (.loid) -> Lexer -> Parser (CST) -> Parser (AST) -> Semantic Analysis And Type Checking -> -> AST Generator (JavaScript AST) -> JavaScript Generator (.js)
```

- **Lexer** - converts the source text into a token stream
- **Parser (CST)** - builds a CST from the tokens
- **Parser (AST)** - builds an AST from the CST (desugaring)
- **Semantic Analysis and Type Checking** - resolves names and validates some language rules
- **JavaScript AST Generator** - generates the JavaScript AST
- **JavaScript Generator** - generates JavaScript from the AST

